> # ONE of their migrations cannot apply to their own base, and it is this one.
> # Measured on m910q 2026-09-08 on a copy of a live install
> # (pyplan/gates/tortoise-reimport-rehearsal-m910q-2026-09-08/): with tw_world
> # imported from sql/base at head 3a8472e, the updater applied 157 world
> # migrations and cancelled the server on 20260903063722_world with
> #   [1062] Duplicate entry '44070' for key 'PRIMARY'   (spell_proc_event)
> # -- a row their base does not hold, so the file collides with itself or a
> # sibling, and a FRESH install at their head cannot start. Their updater is
> # hash-keyed (SHA1 of the file, src/shared/Database/AutoUpdater.cpp), so the
> # copy rewritten here is a new migration to it: applied once, recorded under
> # its own hash, every statement of it run with duplicates skipped rather than
> # the server cancelled. An install that already recorded the original is left
> # alone by this -- its rows are there and IGNORE skips them. The owner refused
> # a blanket rewrite of all 173 (answer 1, 2026-09-08); this is the one that is
> # measured broken, and the line goes the day upstream fixes the file.
> RUN sed -i 's/^\(\s*\)INSERT INTO/\1INSERT IGNORE INTO/' \
>     /opt/tortoise/sql/database_updates/world/20260903063722_world.sql
